WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.680 --> 00:00:01.189 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  foreign  Joel Baker I run American Giants what we  do is find and restore old 1960s  International fiberglass Giants I'm  going to walk you through how my team  restores a muffler man in this case a  Paul Bunyan Muffler Men were large  20-foot advertising Giants that were  made in the early 60s by the  international fiberglass company these  guys were really made to bring attention  to your business so business owners love  to buy them set them up outside and it  would get the family car to pull over on  a road trip this particular Muffler Man  is buried in the back of an old  Campground in Wisconsin that had gone  out of business we're guessing he's  around a 1964 model which would make him  kind of one of the early ones made  [Music]  when I first discovered this guy and got  a good look at him I realized he had  fallen over when they fall forward they  land on their hands and that had pushed  his shoulders out and cracked them  our guys hold the shoulder in place  while another guy will screw small  boards all around the crack to hold it  in place and when we're happy with that  fit then we screw it in and the next  step is we go into fiberglass filler  once the boards are holding the shoulder  in place for this particular crack then  we can go on the inside of the Giant and  we can lay down fresh fiberglass and  basically connect that shoulder piece  back to the rest of the Giant  this bunion head had a lot of detail in  the beard so we basically take Bondo and  fiberglass resin and we put it into all  those seams of the beard  once all the cracks on the giant are  fixed and all the holes are patched it's  really the hardest part of the job and  this is sanding we use orbital sanders  to sand the Giant and we go over every  square inch of the Giant and remove all  those layers of paint that had added up  over the years  once all the fiberglass repair has been  done then it's time for the first coat  of primer and we'd use a filler primer  that's thick and it can seep into all  these little pinholes and cracks and  fill them as best it can  the primer is sprayed onto the whole  Giant  simultaneously while all this priming is  going on we are also working on the feet  the major thing wrong with this guy was  he was missing his feet and this is very  common we see this a lot when you have a  business that has a muffler man and then  they want to get rid of them instead of  unbolting the feet they cut the feet off  at the ankles we have made molds of the  correct feet from other Giants we had to  make new boots  then we find a point on the original  Giant's leg that we can cut and we  basically cut about a foot off of the  original leg at a good mating point for  the new boot we get a good lineup on the  boot into the original leg and then we  use wood and screws to basically bolt  that on and hold it in place once we're  happy with the position and the toes are  pointed in the right direction and  everything looks good then we're going  to go in and we're going to fiberglass  the inside of those legs  and then we'll come back and we'll  actually Prime that area again and make  sure it's a perfect fit and you would  never know that this guy has replacement  feet  in this case the buyer also wanted an ax  that was an original we didn't have an  original ax so we again used our molds  to make an identical ax to the original  axes once the ax is put together then we  go ahead and sand it down and prime it  in anything the Giants hold we always  drill holes in them that way if there  are any water leaks the water can drain  the next step is very tedious but it's  very important and it's called wet  sanding instead of using orbital sanders  we will use sandpaper by hand and we  will spray the giant down so it's all  wet and we'll use a very fine grit of  sandpaper to go over that second coat of  primer and this is basically getting rid  of anything in that primer paint That's  you can't even see but that is sticking  up and may make the final paint job look  not quite as smooth  foreign  once the wet sanding is complete and  everything looks good to us it's an  exciting time because we need to send  all of our pieces a few miles away to a  paint shop once at the paint shop the  painter is going to talk to us about  what colors we want for this giant it's  very important that the buyer picks out  what colors that they want we often will  recommend that they go with the original  colors since we like everything to be  original but it's really up to the buyer  and what paint job they want the first  thing the painter will do is to put on  the base coats on the shoes the legs the  shirt and the head and these are all  solid colors that go on each parts we  always use automotive Quality Paint to  give us a five to seven year life before  you see any signs of deterioration  once the base coats are on the giant  then it's time for the details and this  is when the painter will go to work with  an airbrush and do all the fine details  of highlighting the hair adding skin  tones the painter will also take great  care in doing the eyes and adding a lot  of eye details in the pupils  [Music]  once all the airbrush details are  complete then it is time for a clear  coat and this gives it a really nice  glossy shine and this clear coat also  protects the giant for the long term  from Sun and weather and being in the  elements  [Music]  foreign  start coming back to the shop fully  painted it's the time to really  concentrate on the internal structure of  the giant a lot of the buyers want the  giant to freestand in heavy heavy wind  the first thing we do is we do a  cardboard cutout of the Giant's  footprint and then we take that  cardboard cutout to a machine shop and  they make us a print of that foot that's  made out of a quarter inch steel then we  can weld on four inch pipes onto that  and those pipes will go up the leg once  the pipes reach the waist we weld in  Cross bracing that connects to the waste  it's during the process of installing  the internal structure that we also Bolt  the giant together we do a final fit of  the waste and the Torso and we also bolt  on the head at this time  a date is agreed on with the buyer of  the Giant and before we get there a slab  has been poured at that location once  the giant is unwrapped on site it's time  to install anything that he needs to  hold in his hands with this bunion he  held an ax so we made sure that all the  bolts that the ax were going to slide  onto were already in place so on site  it's pretty easy to just make sure the  fit is right and Bolt the ax down before  he's raised up  at this point a crane or a lift is  brought in to lift the Giant from the  trailer  we take time at this point to make sure  that the giant is orientated properly we  all have the new buyer come in and look  at the Giant and make sure they are a  hundred percent happy with the way the  giant is facing and when they are good  we gently set the giant down on the slab  and those foot plates that are under his  feet they have tabs in them and those  tabs have holes and we basically drill  through those holes into the slab once  all the holes have been drilled we will  insert concrete anchor bolts and once we  tighten those bolts up the bolts expand  underneath in the concrete and press  against the concrete therefore locking  the giant into place  [Music]  once the giant is standing we like to  take a step back and get some pictures  and really take the time to appreciate  all our hard work in bringing another  one of these Giants back to life these  Giants up to this point have lasted for  50 years and we really want to give them  another 50 years  and now this fully restored bunion  stands at Cumberland Ridge Forest  Products in Elkton Kentucky  [Music]  thank you

